A||||6||kevin||Seat bolt replacement||||1||
Z||000000||kevin||09-28-2000||01:21 AM||||I have a stripped bolt(up front)and was wondering what the best procedure would be to remove the threaded bolt for a new one? Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-28-2000||05:55 PM||||I believe those are welded in place. W/o a stud welder you can try to wire weld in a new one or drill a hole and come thru from under the car. If you drill the hole then epoxy the head of the bolt in place and coat it with something to keep it from rusting. Once the epoxy sets it should hold the bolt so you don't have to tray to hold it with a wrench.||||reg||1
Z||000002||286SE'S||09-28-2000||06:09 PM||||Wouldn't it be easier to use a tap and die set to thread the stripped bolt to a smaller size nut?<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||09-28-2000||07:42 PM||||Think you'd be better off replacing the stud. You could try running a die onto the stud, but it's probably not going to be easy getting the die started unless it stripped to the proper size.<P>Or... knock the stud out, turn it down on a lathe, thread it - (wait a minute, as long as it's out), ummm... then replace it with the proper size stud.  I couldn't find the right nut  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> to go with it!        ||||reg||4
Z||000004||pollock||09-28-2000||08:32 PM||||A smaller size thread requires a smaller starting diameter.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-28-2000||09:05 PM||||you'd likely loose alot of strength by rethreading. That could play an issue if you ever have a wreck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||kevin||09-29-2000||08:22 AM||||Thanks fellas for your suggestions. Analyzing the situation, my seat bolt appears welded in. I could not find a way to push it out from under the car. Therefor, I'll find someone to weld in another bolt. Bad design by the boys at Pontiac.||||reg||1
